<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Orange-Nassau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Orange-Nassau is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> royal family, descended from the German house of Nassau, whose member <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a> inherited the Principality of Orange in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1544. As stadtholders its princes led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, and William III also became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Since 1815 the family has provided the monarchs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau.jpg" alt="House of Orange-Nassau" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Evert van Leeuwen (1855-1931) SVG is Own work · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koninklijk_wapen_van_het_koninkrijk_der_Nederlanden.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The princes of <b>Orange-Nassau</b> held the provincial office of stadtholder in most provinces and the command of the army, which gave them a quasi-monarchical standing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Luxembourg was therefore a personal possession of the <b>House of Orange-Nassau</b> rather than part of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, although William for years governed it much like a Dutch province.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom itself had been created in 1815, when the Congress of Vienna united the former Dutch Republic, which the French had turned into the Batavian Republic and then the Kingdom of Holland, with the former Austrian Netherlands under William I of the <b>House of Orange-Nassau</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The States appointed him, and nearly always chose a prince of the <b>House of Orange-Nassau</b>, beginning with William the Silent, so that the office became effectively hereditary.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Lingen then changed hands between Spain and the house of Orange during the Eighty Years' War, and the <b>Oranges</b> founded a Calvinist academy there in 1697.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-lingen">County of Lingen</a></li><li><blockquote>Walram's line produced King Adolf of Nassau, elected in 1292, and later the grand dukes of Luxembourg; Otto's line produced William the Silent and the house of <b>Orange-Nassau</b>, today's Dutch royal family.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau">County of Nassau</a></li></ul></section>
